{"study_accession":"PRJNA689555","secondary_study_accession":"SRP300213","title":"Expression profile of peripheral immune cells-derived coding and long non-coding RNAs in patients with proliferative vitreoretinopathy","description":"Peripheral immune response has been revealed to play a critical role in proliferative vitreoretinopathy (PVR). However, the reliable immune-related factors that are acting as prognostic indicators or therapeutic targets for PVR remain to explore further. Methods: In the current study, we applied whole-transcriptome sequencing to profile peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) from PVR patients and also analyzed lncRNA-mRNA interactions in peripheral immune cells to explore the pathways that might mediate immunopathology and resultant retinal damage in PVR. Gene ontology (GO), Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes (KEGG) pathway enrichment analyses and Ingenuity Pathway Analysis (IPA) were employed to classify the function of these differentially expressed genes (DEGs). Results: Compared to the controls, there were 319 genes upregulated, and 191 genes downregulated in PVR patients. GO, and KEGG enrichment analyses as well as IPA showed that these upregulated genes were significantly enriched in immune-related and infection-relate terms. Immune-related gene NFKBIA, CXCL2, and CXCL8 were detected as hub-genes in the co-expression network, while lncRNAs such as AC007032.1, AC037198.2, AL929472.2, and SLED1 were highly co-expressed with them. lncRNA-mRNA interactions analysis also showed that putative targeted genes of these differentially expressed lncRNAs were also significantly enriched in immune-related or infection-relate pathways. Conclusion: Our study highlights the transformation of immune-related genes/pathways in PVR by comparing controls, and validates several critical genes and lncRNAs, which are serving as potential diagnostic markers for PVR patients. Overall design: peripheral blood mononuclear cells mRNA profiles of proliferative vitreoretinopathy patients and idiopathic epiretinal membrane patients.","pubmed":"33509158","geo_series":"","n_samples":24,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN17209931","study_accession":"PRJNA689555","blood_class":"pbmc","class_from":"sample","title":"1-7","description":"1-7","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM5003944","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2021-01-10","attributes":[["source_name","peripheral blood mononuclear cells"],["nationality","Chinese"],["ethnicity","Han"],["diesase status","idiopathic epiretinal membrane patient"],["cell_type","peripheral blood mononuclear cells"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR13349076","experiment_accession":"SRX9775097","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"HiSeq X Ten","read_count":46621490,"base_count":13986447000,"first_public":"2021-01-10","experiment_title":"HiSeq X Ten sequencing: GSM5003944: 1-7 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN17209932","study_accession":"PRJNA689555","blood_class":"pbmc","class_from":"sample","title":"1-6","description":"1-6","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM5003943","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2021-01-10","attributes":[["source_name","peripheral blood mononuclear cells"],["nationality","Chinese"],["ethnicity","Han"],["diesase status","idiopathic epiretinal membrane patient"],["cell_type","peripheral blood mononuclear cells"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR13349075","experiment_accession":"SRX9775096","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"HiSeq X Ten","read_count":46986716,"base_count":14096014800,"first_public":"2021-01-10","experiment_title":"HiSeq X Ten sequencing: GSM5003943: 1-6 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN17209933","study_accession":"PRJNA689555","blood_class":"pbmc","class_from":"sample","title":"1-5","description":"1-5","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM5003942","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_r